<BaseLayout title="Safe e-waste handling guide" description="A practical, safety-first guide to deciding what to do with unwanted electronics in India.">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">A practical guide</p><h1>What to do with an unwanted device</h1><p class="lede">Keep the device intact and use a formal take-back or recycling route. If you are unsure, check with the product maker or an official environmental authority.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section-tight"><div class="container content-grid">
    <article class="prose">
      <div class="note"><strong>Safety first</strong><p>Do not open, burn, crush, or dismantle electronic devices or batteries. Informal processing can expose people and the environment to harmful substances.</p></div>
      <h2>1. Decide whether it can be reused</h2>
      <p>If it still works, consider repair, reuse, or a responsible handover for reuse before recycling. Remove personal accounts and files before transferring a device.</p>
      <h2>2. Protect your information</h2>
      <ul><li>Back up files you want to keep.</li><li>Sign out of accounts and remove the device from account recovery or device-finding services.</li><li>Use the manufacturer’s reset guidance where possible. Remove SIM and removable storage if applicable.</li><li>Do not share passwords or personal data with an unverified buyer or collector.</li></ul>
      <h2>3. Choose a formal route</h2>
      <ul><li>Check whether the product maker offers a take-back or collection option.</li><li>Use a recycler or collection option listed by the relevant pollution control authority.</li><li>Confirm item acceptance, timings, any charges, and proof of handover directly before travelling.</li></ul>
      <h2>4. Keep waste batteries separate</h2>
      <p>Batteries have a separate regulatory framework in India. Follow the battery take-back instructions from the product maker or the relevant local authority. Do not place loose waste batteries in general rubbish or attempt to open them.</p>
      <h2>If a device is swollen, hot, leaking, or smoking</h2>
      <p>Do not open or handle it. Keep other people away. If there is smoke, fire, or immediate danger, move away and contact emergency services. Ask the product maker or local authority for safe next steps.</p>
      <h2>Before you hand it over</h2>
      <p>Ask for a receipt or other confirmation when available. A listing on a public directory does not guarantee that a facility accepts every item or is currently operating.</p>
      <div class="source-caption"><p><strong>Why this guidance is cautious:</strong> CPCB explains that unsafe opening or disposal can create health and environmental risks. This page is general information, not technical processing advice or legal advice.</p><p><a href="https://cpcb.nic.in/uploads/Projects/E-Waste/FAQ_ewaste_23012024.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the CPCB e-waste FAQ (PDF)</a></p></div>
    </article>
